/-!
# The dispersion bound at `q = 197/130` (this work)

The last affordable member of the family.  `DispersionGeneral` states
the bound once with the constants as parameters; this file supplies
them, and five `decide`s discharge the integer facts.

## Why `197/130`, and why it is the last one

The instance at `q` delivers `c ≥ 2^{1+V(q)}` with
`V(q) = 1/q − log₂ ρ_c(q)`, and `κ* = sup_q V(q)` is attained at
`q* = 1.5153832361…`.  The continued fraction of `q*` is

  `3/2`, `47/31`, `50/33`, **`197/130`**, `8324/5493`, …

and the deficit `2^{1+κ*} − 2^{1+V(q)}` falls like `(q − q*)²`.  So the
convergents are the only exponents worth instantiating, and `197/130`
is the last one whose constants fit: the next, `8324/5493`, would need
`L^r` with about 7·10⁸ digits, against 5·10⁵ here.

## The design

`σ = ρ_c(q)^(1/r)` rounded **up** to `k = 20` decimals, so that
`ρ = σ^r` and `λ = σ^n = L/D` are exactly rational and the rounding is
what leaves the slack `E = L − D − B ≥ 1` that absorbs the `+1` of the
recursion.  Here `σ = 100175122756530307640/10^20`.  `M` only
scales `W`, so it costs nothing in the limit and is taken as large as
the slack needs.  The split fraction `t₁/t_b` is the solution of the
characteristic equation to 21 decimals; at `k = 20` the value below is
already the exponent's limit `2^{1+V(197/130)}` to 19 digits.

The bound this delivers is

  `c ≥ 2·(2D/L)^(r/n) = 2.5170648368936010209…`

against `2.5170648301510146886…` from `q = 50/33` and
`2.5170647740391802928…` from `q = 47/31`: the gap to the exact
`2^(1+κ*) = 2.5170648368938161206…` falls from `6.7·10⁻⁹` to
`2.2·10⁻¹³`.

`Dispersion197130Growth` turns this into a bound on the real number `c`.
-/

